{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "the social network, directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, is a masterful exploration of ambition, betrayal, and the birth of a digital empire. released in 2010, the film dissects the early days of facebook and the turbulent relationships that shaped its creation. with a sharp script and razor-edged dialogue, it blends factual storytelling with cinematic flair, making it both a compelling drama and a biting commentary on human connection in the internet age.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, the harvard student whose genius and social alienation fuel the creation of facebook. his portrayal is cold yet calculating, capturing zuckerberg’s brilliance and emotional detachment. alongside him, andrew garfield shines as eduardo severin, the loyal friend and co-founder whose downfall becomes a tragic arc. the chemistry between the cast—including armie hammer as the winkelvoss twins and max minghella as divya narendra—feels electric, driving the narrative forward with tension and humor.\n\nfincher’s direction is sleek and precise, with a visual style that mirrors the sterile, fast-paced world of tech startups. the film’s pacing is relentless, mirroring the breakneck speed of innovation and backstabbing. the soundtrack, featuring trent reznor and atticus ross’s haunting score, elevates the emotional weight of each scene, from moments of triumph to crushing defeat.\n\nwhat makes the social network more than just a tech drama is its focus on human frailty. beneath the glossy surface of silicon valley lies a story of envy, greed, and the cost of success. the film doesn’t glorify zuckerberg; instead, it presents him as a flawed, complex figure whose creation both connects and isolates.\n\nthe social network is a triumph of modern cinema—smart, stylish, and endlessly quotable. it’s a film that lingers long after the credits roll, forcing viewers to question the true cost of innovation and the relationships we sacrifice along the way. a modern classic that remains as relevant today as it was over a decade ago.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.3156192898750305}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.003436019876971841}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.066009998321533}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -0.011736157350242138}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -4.529942543740617e-06}, {"token": " dav", "logprob": -2.038458114839159e-05}, {"token": "id", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.00014506718434859067}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.038224782794713974}, {"token": " written", "logprob": -0.002102547325193882}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -1.7881377516459906e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00022575691400561482}, {"token": "aron", "logprob": -2.074220174108632e-05}, {"token": " s", "logprob": -2.90866428258596e-05}, {"token": "ork", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0040123919025063515}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.0002585315378382802}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.001591607229784131}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -1.231829047203064}, {"token": "ful", "logprob": -0.7756484746932983}, {"token": " exploration", "logprob": -0.11535202711820602}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.58813522325363e-05}, {"token": " ambition", "logprob": -0.1909361034631729}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920858014491387e-05}, {"token": " betray", "logprob": -0.009136770851910114}, {"token": "al", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.8954096958623268e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.0002821285743266344}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0007981451926752925}, {"token": " birth", "logprob": -0.26845622062683105}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -7.712543447